    "https://newhaven.hrmdirect.com/employment/job-opening.php?req=3801787&&#job": {
      "title": "Manager Military Veterans Affairs",
      "description": "Manager Military Veterans Affairs at University of New Haven Skip to jobs Back to Job Openings Manager Military Veterans Affairs Job Location West Haven, CT, US Apply Share Link copied Who we are: The University of New Haven, founded in 1920, is a private university whose mission is to prepare students to excel and lead purposeful and fulfilling lives in a global society. The university offers more than 150 undergraduate and graduate programs and has been recognized for academic excellence, nationally and internationally. In addition to its main campus in West Haven, the university has a campus in Tuscany, Italy. Learn more at newhaven.edu . The Manager, Military & Veteran Affairs provides comprehensive, student-centered support for military-affiliated students, including active-duty service members, reservists, National Guard members, veterans, military and veteran dependents, and ROTC cadets. The Manager serves as the primary campus resource from inquiry through graduation for questions related to recruitment, enrollment, military and veteran educational benefits, regulatory compliance, referrals, engagement, and student success. The position leads the Office of Military & Veteran Affairs, manages the Veteran Center and VA Work Study program, and collaborates across the University to strengthen recruitment, retention, communication, compliance, and community-building initiatives. You will: Compliance, Certification and Benefits Ensure compliance with applicable U.S. Department of Veterans Affairs, Department of Defense, and state requirements, working with the State Approving Agency and Education Liaison Representative. Serve as the primary School Certifying Official and submit enrollment certifications for VA education benefits. Assist students with benefit eligibility and application processes, and maintain benefit information in applicable VA, DOD, and University systems, including the VA Enrollment Manager and related portals. Monitor legislative, regulatory, and policy changes affecting military-affiliated students and communicate pertinent updates to relevant campus partners. Serve as the primary point of contact for disbursement of the Veterans Emergency Loan Fund under Bursar supervision. Student Support and Enrollment Serve as the focal point of contact from inquiry through graduation for military-affiliated students seeking guidance on educational benefits, compliance, University resources, and campus points of contact. Serve as primary enrollment recruiter for military-affiliated students and support undergraduate and graduate recruitment efforts, recruitment events, outreach initiatives, and military- or veteran-related college fairs as needed. Partner with Student Success and Student Affairs to orient students to University life, monitor academic progress, make referrals, and share appropriate concerns through Navigate. Coordinate an outreach plan with Student Affairs and the One Stop Office to maximize communication and support for all military-affiliated students, regardless of VA educational-benefit coverage. Maintain working knowledge of Admissions, Advising, Financial Aid, Bursar, and Registrar operations and procedures to provide informed referrals and coordinated support. Office, Program and Community Leadership Manage the Office of Military & Veteran Affairs and the campus Veteran Center. Recruit, hire, supervise, schedule, and support participants in the VA Work Study program. Serve as Service to Scholar Program Director. Maintain and co-chair the Veterans Advisory Board, convene recurring meetings, and collaborate on community-building initiatives. Plan and execute the annual Salute to Service Awards Banquet celebrating student veterans and community partners. Develop and deliver University-sponsored Green Zone Training for pertinent departments and student leaders. Distribute the OMVA newsletter to military-affiliated students and, when appropriate, the larger campus community, including timely VA updates and University information. Manage military and veteran program budgets and related resources. Partnerships, Engagement and Institutional Support Serve as the University liaison for ROTC and the SALUTE Veterans National Honor Society. Advise the Charger Vets student organization and support academic, service, community-building, and recreational programs for military-affiliated students. Coordinate and lead meetings for faculty and staff involved in supporting military-affiliated students. Manage and submit surveys related to military-friendly and best-for-veterans recognition efforts in collaboration with Institutional Research. Provide staff with resources that promote excellent service and coordinated military and veteran support across campus. Support the goals and mission of Enrollment and Student Success and the University. Perform other duties and responsibilities as assigned by supervisor and Enrollment Vice President that are in accordance with the goals and mission of the University. You need: Bachelor's degree and two to four years of relevant experience. Prior service in the United States Armed Forces. Valid driver’s license and ability to travel on behalf of the University. Availability to work non-traditional hours, including occasional early mornings, evenings, and weekends. Knowledge of and demonstrated appreciation for the experiences and needs of individuals from military and veteran backgrounds. Strong interpersonal, written, and verbal communication skills, with a professional presentation and demeanor. Ability to safeguard sensitive and private information and maintain strict confidentiality. Good judgment, tact, diplomacy, discretion, reliability, flexibility, and consistent follow-through. Excellent problem-solving, organizational, and customer-service skills. Ability to work independently, collaborate effectively across departments, contribute as a team member, and meet deadlines. Proficiency with Microsoft 365 applications, including Word, Excel, Outlook, and Teams. Experience working in a higher education environment preferred. What's in it for you: Health & Welfare Programs: Employees and their eligible family members, including spouses and dependent are eligible for Medical, Dental, Vision and Life Programs. Wellness & Fitness: On-campus Recreation and Fitness Center for employees and families; Employee Assistant Program Commitment to Educational Excellence: Tuition assistance for employees and eligible dependents; Tuition exchange opportunities Employee Discounts on products, services and educational opportunities Work/Life Benefits: Generous paid time off programs; progressive paid holiday schedule (including holiday break between Christmas and New Year’s); 4 weeks of parental paid leave; flexible work schedules including summer hours where applicable *The University of New Haven is not a qualifying E-Verify employer and its use of E-Verify is limited to specific government projects. For that reason, candidates with STEM OPT authorization cannot be considered for employment. © 2026 University of New Haven | Visit our home page Applicant tracking system powered by Powered by"
    },
    "https://newhaven.hrmdirect.com/employment/job-opening.php?req=3798678&&#job": {
      "title": "Career Advisor at University of New Haven",
      "description": "Career Advisor at University of New Haven Skip to jobs Back to Job Openings Career Advisor Job Location West Haven, CT, US Apply Share Link copied Who we are: The University of New Haven, founded in 1920, is a private university whose mission is to prepare students to excel and lead purposeful and fulfilling lives in a global society. The university offers more than 150 undergraduate and graduate programs and has been recognized for academic excellence, nationally and internationally. In addition to its main campus in West Haven, the university has a campus in Tuscany, Italy. Learn more at newhaven.edu . The Career Advisor will serve as a lead in developing career programming and conducting career preparation activities for students and alumni including workshops, resume/cover letter services, collaborative presentations/programs, mock interviews, and internship/job search training. Collaborate with college and program faculty, student affairs, and internal department colleagues to create customized training programs for students in particular colleges and/or career fields. Provide individual appointments and group assistance to students and alumni on all career development related needs. Support office events and operations such as career fairs, networking events, alumni engagement, admissions events, retention initiatives, and student life programs. You will: Serve as subject matter expert for internships, careers, and career readiness initiatives for students. Understand and effectively communicate hiring standards, practices, and related information for these programs and associated industries. Develop a deep knowledge of careers and industries available to students and alumni in these programs. Lead departmental development and execution of yearly programming related to career management and preparation services for students and alumni including workshop development on resume/cover letter services, mock interviews, and internship/job search training. Build collaborative relationships with faculty, academic support, and student affairs colleagues to ensure adequate programs and resources to meet customized needs of students and alumni with a wide variety of career interests. Develop targeted programming to prepare students for the job/internship search (resumes/cover letters, interviews, job offers, salary information, etc.) Collaborate with various student groups and departments to execute career development programs. Lead the creation and execution of creative programming to engage students. Work individually with students and alumni on career development related needs including career exploration, resume/cover letter development, job/internship searches, interviewing assistance, and graduate school preparation. Provide leadership and support to “Application Ready” initiatives including course delivery, content creation, resource management, and execution of events/programs to ensure students are ready to apply to partnership opportunities. Meet with and coach students (undergraduate and graduate) and alumni on a variety of skill and personality assessment programs to develop individualized career development plans. Utilize Handshake career management system for appointment setting and notes, career resource development, event management, and student engagement. Actively connect students and alumni with resources on career and graduate school options such as written and web-based materials, informational interviews, faculty, staff, etc. Support academic program initiatives, industry engagement, grants and sponsorships, and related activities to build effective knowledge of academic programs and opportunities. SECONDARY DUTIES: Develop and maintain knowledge of local, regional, and national hiring trends as well as employer preferences for hiring college graduates. Develop and maintain knowledge of graduate schools, application trends, and testing requirements. Maintain required advising notes, data metrics, and submit reports as required. Utilize numerous technologies including Handshake, resume and interview software, career assessments, and internal University technology for daily office operations and education programs. Other duties as assigned You need: Bachelor’s degree AND at least 2-3 years of related student counseling, coaching, or advising experience, preferably in a college career center OR appropriate combination of education and work experience. Experience working with students and alumni on career development needs including resumes, job searching, internships, interviewing, graduate school preparation, portfolios, and other career-related information and resources. Ability to learn and convey subject matter expertise is required. Knowledge of career planning theory, practices, and techniques including career assessment instruments. Ability to collaborate with faculty and staff on academic and career planning programs. Knowledge of issues pertaining to first-generation college students, graduate students, international students, mid-career changers, and senior level alumni. Skill in both verbal and written communication. Ability to handle multiple tasks and work with constant interruptions. Comfortable working in an open office environment. Proficiency with technology including Handshake, MS Office, online research, online career development tools, and other office technology What's in it for you: Health & Welfare Programs: Employees and their eligible family members, including spouses and dependent are eligible for Medical, Dental, Vision and Life Programs. Wellness & Fitness: On-campus Recreation and Fitness Center for employees and families; Employee Assistant Program Commitment to Educational Excellence: Tuition assistance for employees and eligible dependents; Tuition exchange opportunities Employee Discounts on products, services and educational opportunities Work/Life Benefits: Generous paid time off programs; progressive paid holiday schedule (including holiday break between Christmas and New Year’s); 4 weeks of parental paid leave; flexible work schedules including summer hours where applicable *The University of New Haven is not a qualifying E-Verify employer and its use of E-Verify is limited to specific government projects. For that reason, candidates with STEM OPT authorization cannot be considered for employment. © 2026 University of New Haven | Visit our home page Applicant tracking system powered by Powered by"
